Aim:
To create and understand different basic graphs and visualizations using R programming.

Algorithm:
 Create the required R vectors.
 Select a suitable visualization function.
 Pass the data to the graph function.
 Add title and axis labels.
 Execute the R program.
 Observe and interpret the graph.

Program 1: Line Graph

 x <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
 y <- c(10, 15, 12, 18, 20)
 plot(x, y, type="o",
 main="Line Graph",
 xlab="X Values",
 ylab="Y Values")

Program 2: Bar Chart

 subjects <- c("Python", "Java", "R", "Tableau", "SQL")
 marks <- c(85, 78, 82, 90, 88)
 barplot(marks,
 names.arg=subjects,
 main="Marks by Subject",
 xlab="Subject",
 ylab="Marks")

Program 3: Pie Chart

 values <- c(35, 25, 20, 20)
 labels <- c("Python", "Java", "R", "Tableau")
 pie(values,
 labels=labels,
 main="Technology Usage")

Program 4: Scatter Plot

 hours <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)
 marks <- c(45, 50, 58, 65, 72, 80)
 plot(hours, marks,
 main="Study Hours vs Marks",
 xlab="Study Hours",
 ylab="Marks",
 pch=19)

Program 5: Histogram

 marks <- c(45, 50, 52, 55, 58, 60, 62, 65,
 68, 70, 72, 75, 78, 80, 82, 85,
 88, 90)
 hist(marks,
 breaks=5,
 main="Distribution of Marks",xlab="Marks",
 ylab="Frequency")

Result:
Thus, five basic visualizations—line graph, bar chart, pie chart, scatter plot and histogram—were
successfully created using R programming.
